KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp14 --object-bits 12 --unwind 8 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ init-capture by move: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[expr.prim.lambda.capture]/6, /15: an init-capture `q = std::move(p)'
declares a member initialized as `auto q = std::move(p)', by unique_ptr's
move constructor, which leaves p null.  CBMC deduced the member's type
from the initializer without removing its reference (so the closure held
the address of p), and initialized class-type members of a closure as a
value without calling a constructor; p and the closure's member then both
owned the int, and the second destructor reported "double delete".  Now
the init-capture's type is deduced as for auto ([dcl.type.auto.deduct]/3)
and class-type members are direct-initialized by their constructors.
